{:edb/db-before-t 2430 :edb/db-after-t 2431 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535743 50 #inst "2026-10-09T06:26:30.559Z" 13194139535743 true] [17592186364277 1100 "Which of the following random variables follow a geometric distribution?\n\n- The number of files reviewed by an antivirus until the first infected file is found\n- The number of infected files found in a computer\n- The number of crayons randomly picked out from a case before a blue crayon is picked" 13194139535743 false] [17592186364277 1100 "Which of the following random variables follow a geometric distribution?\n\n- The number of files reviewed by an antivirus until the first infected file is found\n- The number of infected files found in a computer\n- The number of crayons randomly picked out from a case before a blue crayon is picked\n\nAssume independent trials with a constant success probability strictly between $0$ and $1$ for each experiment. Replace and mix each crayon before the next draw." 13194139535743 true] [17592186364277 1145 "A geometric distribution is used to model the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I follows a geometric distribution. Reviewing a file is a binomial experiment, and the number of files reviewed before the first infected file is found can be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\n- Random variable II does *not* follow a geometric distribution. Finding an infected file is a binomial experiment, but the number of infected files in a computer cannot be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\n- Random variable III follows a geometric distribution. Randomly picking out a crayon is a binomial experiment, and the number of crayons picked out before a blue crayon is picked can be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139535743 false] [17592186364277 1145 "A geometric distribution models waiting for the first success in independent Bernoulli trials with constant success probability.\n\n- I counts file checks through the first infected file. Under the stated model, $P(T=k)=(1-p)^{k-1}p$ for $k=1,2,\\ldots$.\n- II counts the total infected files in a computer; it does not count trials until the first success.\n- III counts nonblue draws before the first blue crayon. Replacement keeps the probability of blue constant. Under the stated model, $P(F=k)=(1-q)^kq$ for $k=0,1,2,\\ldots$.\n\nI and III are geometric, using the trials and failures conventions respectively. The correct answer is \"I and III only.\"\n\nThe original source omits the independence and constant-probability assumptions and does not specify crayon replacement. Without replacement, III generally is not geometric; the study wording makes the intended model explicit." 13194139535743 true]] :edb/tempids {} :edb/committed false :edb/speculative true}
